A related problem is the choice of editor for each scenario.  Due to the 
> nature of Windows, you can have different editors for different purposes. 
>  For example, whether Notepad is generally for you or not, there is a 
> function where you probably wouldn't change from Notepad.
>

In any scenario BATCH file [run by a designated ICON in the command window, 
you have to specify the program and its path.  However, Windows System 
programs enjoy special treatment; just use them by name.  Thus, to add on 
another editor, you need to know a little bit more information.  Using 
environment variable tricks, these parameters have been separated out as 
well as possible, but you still have to know a moderate amount of how 
Windows works at this level.

There is the concept of the official current directory; this is quite 
useful for quick programs or for those without ornate directory needs. 
 [There are some other features to move back and forth to?from complex 
directory structures with an official recommendation.  Perhaps that's for 
some, something for when there is more familiarity with the package, but 
for some it won't apply.  Regardless it is recommended to use the current 
directory when getting started.

The current directory has some special maintenance functions such as 
setting the current filename.  There is also an icon to obtain a complete 
listing of the files in the current directory.  This is created by Notepad 
for several reasons:

Some editors use a tabbed structure.  It could become unweildy to have a 
brief usage for a listing of the directory, because that can allow the user 
to set a particular current file that already exists, or is somewhat 
similar to an existing filename.  Since the Notepad window can use the copy 
and paste options directly, the name as displayed can help to define a new 
current directory filename when the function Set Current Directory Filename 
is used.  Unless and until changed, this name becomes permanent and is 
stored within an internal parameter file.  If the package is repackaged for 
transport to another system, the current filename particulars will apply. 
 As released, the value of the current directory filename is HELLOWORLD.PAL 
and the corresponding file is in the current directory.  [The name explains 
what it is, the assembly language source file for something that prints out 
HELLO WORLD on the PDP-8 device 04 system console.]

Often names are created with an implied sequence such as PRG1 then PRG2 
etc.  Using the Notepad output of the List Current Directory icon, copying 
and pasting can same a bit of typing into the new filename diagloue box 
which pops up when the Set Current Program Filename function is chosen and 
the question asked to change the name after displaying the current value, 
etc.  Thus, the entire filename PRG1.PAL could be copied, pasted and then 
backspaced over to change the 1 to the 2.  All of the standard name change 
functions apply as this little window is a graphical window and not a 
typical DOS-like batch window, etc.

There is also an Explorer Current Directory function which is of course 
Windows Explorer, and there are other icons to open various other 
directories.  This would allow typical two-explorer window cross copying, 
etc. where the windows are opened to the likely choices without any major 
navigation, etc.

Ultimate, the chosen current directory filename can be designed to be 
editied with a designated editor, and then with yet another icon, it can be 
sent to SIMH via the PTR: mechanism, which copies the filo being edited 
into the designated internal file of SIMH which will be used for the 
paper-tape reader input when and if.

In OS/8 this must be handled with PIP writing an output file from the PTR: 
input.

In P?S/8, the new PTUTIL program serves the P/S/8 equivalent function.

Each SEND icon can choose the operating system on SIMH of choice; this 
requires a little Windows training to understand how to make minor edits to 
designated batch files, but there is documentation on how to modify a 
typical send-type batch file.  The changes to be made are minimal and 
should be obvious from the internal comments in the batch files, etc.

Much of PEPS can and should be customized for personal preference, but the 
process has been organized for minimal effort to change.

For editing any file, either Notepad is recommended for independent 
windows, or editpadlite7 for a tabbed editor.  Not every editing program is 
compatible with PEPS.  Documentation will be available as to how to 
determine the ones that work.  The basic requirement is to be able to 
handle ASCII text 7-bit characters including the <FF> character directlly, 
and to be able to be run from a commmand-line with the passed ASCII 
filename as an inline argument.  Perhaps as many as 40% of editors are 
compatible, and a surprising number are not so.

cjl

